<?xml version='1.0' encoding='UTF-8'?>
<chapter xml:id="grv-ch-zero-divisors">
  <title>Zero divisors and failed cancellation</title>
  <introduction xml:id="grv-sec-regularity">
    <p>Can multiplication by a nonempty variety be cancelled in <m>\Rk</m>? The stable birational quotient gives a way to prove that a virtual difference is nonzero; a geometric product isomorphism can then show that a variety annihilates it. This chapter develops that argument first for abelian varieties and then for the affine line.</p>
    <p xml:id="grv-def-zero-divisor">An element <m>a</m> of a commutative ring <m>R</m> is <term>regular</term>, or a <term>non-zero-divisor</term>, if multiplication <m>R\xrightarrow{a}R</m> is injective. A nonzero <m>a</m> is a <term>zero divisor</term> if some nonzero <m>b</m> satisfies <m>ab=0</m>.<idx>zero divisor</idx><idx>non-zero-divisor</idx><idx>regular element</idx></p><p>These definitions concern all virtual classes. For nonempty varieties <m>X,Y</m>, one always has <m>[X][Y]=[X\times Y]\ne0</m>, by <xref ref="grv-prop-nonempty"/>. Hence an annihilator of a nonempty variety cannot itself be the class of a nonempty variety. It can be a difference of two such classes.</p>
    <p>The problem is therefore to construct an annihilating relation and prove that both factors are nonzero. The quotient modulo <m>\LL</m> will supply the first nonvanishing arguments. After <m>\LL</m> is inverted, the affine-line example disappears and the abelian examples require a finer realization.</p>
  </introduction>
  <section xml:id="grv-sec-abelian-zero-divisors">
    <title>Abelian varieties and product cancellation</title>
    <theorem xml:id="grv-thm-poonen">
      <title>Poonen</title>
      <statement><p>There exist nonisomorphic abelian varieties <m>A,B</m> over <m>k</m> such that <m>A\times A\simeq B\times B</m>. Consequently,
        <md number="no">([A]-[B])([A]+[B])=0,</md>
        and both factors are nonzero modulo <m>\LL</m>. In particular, <m>\Rk</m> is not a domain, and the class of the single, disconnected variety <m>A\sqcup B</m> is a zero divisor.</p></statement>
      <proof><p>The arithmetic construction in <xref ref="grv-bib-Poonen" detail="Lemmas 2 and 3"/> starts with an abelian variety over <m>\Q</m> whose geometric endomorphism ring is a suitable Dedekind domain <m>\mathcal O</m>, and a nonprincipal invertible ideal <m>I</m> with class of order two. The associated abelian variety <m>B=I\otimes_{\mathcal O}A</m> is not isomorphic to <m>A</m>, while <m>I\oplus I\simeq\mathcal O\oplus\mathcal O</m> gives the asserted product isomorphism. The existence of this arithmetic input is an external result here.</p>
        <p>The product isomorphism gives the displayed ring relation after extension to <m>k</m>. The Albanese criterion shows that <m>A,B</m> have distinct stable birational classes. Their difference is therefore nonzero modulo <m>\LL</m>. Their sum is a sum of two basis elements in <m>\Z[\SB_k]</m>, so it too is nonzero modulo <m>\LL</m>.</p></proof>
    </theorem>
    <p>Thus zero divisors are not confined to the ideal generated by <m>\LL</m>. This example also demonstrates why being projective does not ensure regularity. The following explicit calculation removes the disconnectedness of the zero-divisor variety.</p>
    <proposition xml:id="grv-ex-shioda-elliptic">
      <title>A smooth projective irreducible zero divisor</title>
      <statement><p>Work over <m>\C</m> and put
        <md>
          <mrow>O=\Z+i\Z,\qquad O_3=\Z+3i\Z,\qquad I=2\Z+(1+3i)\Z,</mrow>
          <mrow>E=\C/O,\qquad E_1=\C/O_3,\qquad E_2=\C/I.</mrow>
        </md>
        These are elliptic curves. One has <m>E\times E_1\simeq E\times E_2</m> but <m>E_1\not\simeq E_2</m>. Therefore
        <md number="no">[E]([E_1]-[E_2])=0,\qquad [E_1]-[E_2]\ne0.</md>
        The elliptic curve <m>E</m> is a smooth projective irreducible variety whose class is a zero divisor, and <m>[E]\notin(\LL)</m>.</p></statement>
      <proof><p>Consider the complex linear map given by
        <md number="no">F=\begin{pmatrix}1+i&amp;i\\3(1+i)&amp;1+3i\end{pmatrix}.</md>
        Its columns show that <m>F(O\oplus O_3)\subseteq O\oplus I</m>. For the second coordinate this follows directly from
        <md number="no">I=\{x+3yi:x,y\in\Z,\ x\equiv y\pmod2\}.</md>
        The determinant is <m>1+i</m>. The covolumes of the domain and target lattices are respectively <m>3</m> and <m>6</m>; a complex linear map multiplies real covolume by the squared absolute value of its determinant. Hence the inclusion of lattices has index one. The induced isomorphism of complex tori is algebraic, giving the product isomorphism.</p>
        <p>The shortest nonzero vector in <m>O_3</m> has length <m>1</m>, whereas the shortest in <m>I</m> has length <m>2</m>. If these lattices were homothetic, their areas would force the homothety to have absolute value <m>\sqrt2</m>, which is incompatible with those shortest lengths. Thus the elliptic curves are nonisomorphic. Their difference is nonzero modulo <m>\LL</m> by <xref ref="grv-prop-abelian-sb"/>. The class of <m>E</m> itself maps to a stable birational basis element and is also nonzero modulo <m>\LL</m>.</p></proof>
    </proposition>
    <p>This is an explicit instance of the failure of product cancellation for elliptic curves studied by Shioda; see <xref ref="grv-bib-Shioda"/> and <xref ref="grv-bib-ShinderSlides"/>. The lattice calculation exhibits the common factor, while the Albanese variety distinguishes the two remaining factors in the stable birational quotient.</p>
    <p xml:id="grv-anchor-shioda-picard">The same example explains why a different detector is needed after localization. In the group completion of the monoid of abelian varieties under product, the isomorphism gives <m>[E]+[E_1]=[E]+[E_2]</m>, so <m>[E_1]-[E_2]</m> becomes zero. A realization with this target therefore loses the witness that proved <m>[E]</m> a zero divisor.</p>
    <p xml:id="margin-grv-shioda-picard">Thus the stable birational argument proves nonvanishing in the original ring; it supplies no conclusion about this witness after <m>\LL</m> is inverted. The Picard realization and its cancellation relations are developed in <xref ref="grm-picard-cancellation" text="custom">the Picard realization</xref>.</p>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="grv-sec-affine-line-zero-divisor">
    <title>Affine line and a geometric annihilator</title>
    <p>Borisov proved that <m>\LL</m> is a zero divisor, using Pfaffian–Grassmannian Calabi–Yau threefolds <xref ref="grv-bib-Borisov"/>. A subsequent construction using <m>G_2</m>-Grassmannians gives a particularly strong form: the annihilated difference is itself nonzero modulo <m>\LL</m>.</p>
    <theorem xml:id="grv-thm-imou">
      <title>Ito–Miura–Okawa–Ueda</title>
      <statement><p>There exist smooth projective Calabi–Yau threefolds <m>X,Y</m> over <m>k</m> for which
        <md number="no">\LL([X]-[Y])=0,\qquad [X]-[Y]\notin(\LL).</md>
        In particular, both <m>\LL</m> and <m>[X]-[Y]</m> are zero divisors.</p></statement>
    </theorem>
    <p>The construction and nonvanishing assertion are <xref ref="grv-bib-IMOU" detail="Theorems 1.1 and 1.3"/>. Here is the ring calculation, with its geometric inputs separated. Let <m>F_1,F_2</m> be the two five-dimensional homogeneous spaces <m>G_2/P_i</m>. Their Bruhat decompositions give
      <md number="no">[F_1]=[F_2]=1+\LL+\cdots+\LL^5.</md>
      The complete flag variety has projective-line bundle projections <m>p_i</m> to these spaces. If <m>H_i</m> is the ample generator on <m>F_i</m>, a general divisor of the line bundle <m>p_1^*H_1\otimes p_2^*H_2</m> on the flag variety is simultaneously a blowup
      <md number="no">D\simeq\Bl_XF_1\simeq\Bl_YF_2</md>
      along smooth codimension-two centers. Applying the blowup formula twice yields
      <md number="no">[D]=[F_1]+\LL[X]=[F_2]+\LL[Y],</md>
      which proves the annihilation relation.</p>
    <p>The construction of the smooth centers, their Picard rank one, and the intersection numbers of their ample generators are geometric inputs from the cited paper. Those intersection numbers are <m>42</m> and <m>14</m>. They distinguish the polarized varieties; Picard rank one and the minimal-model argument rule out birationality. The non-uniruled stable birational criterion then rules out stable birationality. Larsen–Lunts therefore proves <m>[X]-[Y]\notin(\LL)</m>. Together with <m>\LL\ne0</m> from <xref ref="grv-prop-nonempty"/>, this proves that the displayed relation has two nonzero factors.</p>
    <corollary xml:id="grv-cor-l-ideal">
      <statement><p>Every nonzero element of the ideal <m>(\LL)\subset\Rk</m> is a zero divisor. Moreover <m>\Ann(\LL)</m> contains an element outside <m>(\LL)</m>, of infinite additive order.</p></statement>
      <proof><p>Put <m>\alpha=[X]-[Y]</m> as in the theorem. For any <m>r</m>, one has <m>(\LL r)\alpha=0</m>, with <m>\alpha\ne0</m>. Its reduction modulo <m>\LL</m> is the difference of two distinct basis elements of <m>\Z[\SB_k]</m>, and therefore has infinite additive order.</p></proof>
    </corollary>
    <corollary xml:id="grv-cor-cut-paste">
      <title>The cut-and-paste question has a negative answer</title>
      <statement><p>Equality of classes in <m>\Rk</m> does not imply piecewise isomorphism, even for smooth integral varieties.</p></statement>
      <proof><p>The varieties <m>X\times\A^1</m> and <m>Y\times\A^1</m> in the theorem have equal classes. Piecewise isomorphic integral varieties have isomorphic dense open subsets, hence are birational. Birationality of these products would imply stable birationality of <m>X,Y</m>, contrary to the theorem.</p></proof>
    </corollary>
    <p>This consequence concerns additive cancellation in the scissors semiring, whereas the elliptic example concerns multiplication by a fixed variety. Neither counterexample says that the individual Calabi–Yau classes <m>[X]</m> and <m>[Y]</m> are zero divisors.</p>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="grv-sec-localized-zero-divisors">
    <title>What localization removes and what it retains</title>
    <p>In <m>\Mk=\Rk[\LL^{-1}]</m>, the relation <m>\LL([X]-[Y])=0</m> becomes <m>[X]=[Y]</m>. More generally, localization kills exactly the elements annihilated by some power of <m>\LL</m>. Inverting the affine line therefore removes this zero divisor, but leaves a further question: do the abelian-variety product relations still have nonzero factors?</p>
    <theorem xml:id="grv-thm-ekedahl-localized">
      <title>Zero divisors survive inversion of the affine line</title>
      <statement><p>The ring <m>\Mk</m> is not a domain. More precisely, the abelian-variety factors in Poonen's construction can both be proved nonzero after inverting <m>\LL</m>.</p></statement>
    </theorem>
    <p>This is a consequence of <xref ref="grv-bib-Ekedahl" detail="Corollary 3.5"/>, which proves nonvanishing in still further localizations and a completion using a refined realization. We use that nonvanishing as a black box, described in <xref ref="grm-picard-cancellation" text="custom">the Picard realization</xref>. Nonvanishing modulo <m>\LL</m> would not by itself suffice: the Ito–Miura–Okawa–Ueda difference is a counterexample to that inference.</p>
    <p>The ideal of <m>\LL</m>-power torsion and the kernel of localization will be treated systematically in <xref ref="grv-leq-localization"/>. Structural descriptions of <m>\Ann(\LL)</m> also involve spectral sequences and birational maps <xref ref="grv-bib-Zakharevich"/>. These address annihilators of the affine line; even the regularity of another specified polynomial in <m>\LL</m> asks for further information.</p>
  </section>
<section xml:id="grv-sec-regularity-tests"><title>Regularity beyond the affine-line ideal</title><p>Every unit is regular, so the class of a point can be cancelled. For <m>n\ge1</m>, a disjoint union of <m>n</m> points has class <m>n</m>; cancelling it is equivalent to the absence of additive <m>n</m>-torsion. More generally, a nonzero Hodge polynomial proves that a class is nonzero but need not detect every possible annihilator.</p><p>For the classes <m>1+\LL=[\PP^1]</m> and <m>\LL-1=[\mathbb G_m]</m>, an annihilator would have to behave differently from the differences killed by powers of <m>\LL</m>. Let <m>T_\infty=\bigcup_{n\ge1}\Ann(\LL^n)</m>, the ideal of such power-torsion elements. The following calculation excludes this entire ideal as a source of annihilators for polynomials with unit constant term.</p><proposition xml:id="grv-prop-unit-constant">
      <statement><p>Let <m>f(t)\in\Z[t]</m> satisfy <m>f(0)=\pm1</m>. Then
        <md number="no">\Ann(f(\LL))\subseteq\bigcap_{n\ge1}\LL^n\Rk,\qquad \Ann(f(\LL))\cap T_\infty=0.</md>
        In particular, multiplication by <m>f(\LL)</m> is injective on the ideal <m>T_\infty</m> of <m>\LL</m>-power torsion.</p></statement>
      <proof><p>Write <m>f(\LL)=\epsilon+\LL g(\LL)</m>, with <m>\epsilon=\pm1</m>. If <m>f(\LL)a=0</m>, then <m>a=-\epsilon\LL g(\LL)a</m>. Iterating shows <m>a\in\LL^n\Rk</m> for every <m>n</m>. If also <m>\LL^Na=0</m>, choose a polynomial <m>h(t)</m> for which <m>h(t)f(t)\equiv1\pmod{t^N}</m>; the truncated geometric series constructs one because <m>f(0)</m> is a unit. Multiplication by <m>a</m> gives <m>a=h(\LL)f(\LL)a=0</m>.</p></proof>
    </proposition>
    <p>The iteration in the proof concerns divisibility by <m>\LL</m> in the original ring, whereas <m>T_\infty</m> concerns annihilation by its powers. In particular, any nonzero annihilator of <m>[\PP^1]</m> or <m>[\mathbb G_m]</m> would survive in <m>\Mk</m> and lie in every ideal <m>\LL^n\Rk</m>. The regularity questions in <xref ref="grv-q-unit-constant"/> start from this restriction.</p></section></chapter>
